Chapter: ListIndexBounds
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `list-at`, `list-set-at` and `list-insert-at` trap on an index outside the
 list. This file is the other half: every LEGAL index must still answer, for
 all three, because the compiler reads `list-at` constantly and a guard that
 refuses a legal read would stop the compiler rather than a program.

 The traps themselves cannot be pinned here -- they are UD2, so the guest
 dies and there is no output to compare. `codex/test/list-oob-read.codex`
 and `codex/test/list-oob-write.codex` are those demonstrations and they
 carry a `.fatal`, which means each is a compile check plus a manual read.
 This file is the part a battery can fail on, so it is the one that has to
 be complete.

 Two boundaries are named because they point in opposite directions.
 `list-insert-at xs (list-length xs) v` is an APPEND and is legal -- the
 shift loop moves nothing and the store lands in the slot the length is
 about to grow into -- so the insert bound is `i <= len` where the read and
 write bounds are `i < len`. A guard copied from one to the other without
 reading the shift loop gets exactly this wrong, and `ins-end` below is the
 line that says so.

 The view rows -- a tail view is bounded by its own length, not the
 backing block's -- lived here until 2026-07-28 and are now
 `list-view-bounds`. The tail view comes from a Cons match, which the
 cross plugs refuse by design, and while the rows lived here the refusal
 took this file's six legal-index readings off both cross lanes with
 them. Split, this file runs everywhere the compiler does.

Section: Report

  walk-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  walk-at (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else walk-at xs (i + 1) n (acc & " " & show (list-at xs i))

  read-all : List Integer -> Text
  read-all (xs) = walk-at xs 0 (list-length xs) ""

  set-walk : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  set-walk (xs) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then xs
    else set-walk (list-set-at xs i (i * 10)) (i + 1) n

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("read:" & read-all [1, 2, 3, 4])
    print-line-uni ("last=" & show (list-at [1, 2, 3, 4] 3))
    print-line-uni ("set:" & read-all (set-walk [1, 2, 3, 4] 0 4))
    print-line-uni ("ins-front:" & read-all (list-insert-at [1, 2, 3] 0 99))
    print-line-uni ("ins-mid:" & read-all (list-insert-at [1, 2, 3] 1 99))
    print-line-uni ("ins-end:" & read-all (list-insert-at [1, 2, 3] 3 99))
  end

